--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
You wake on a straw-stuffed mattress that crackles under your weight. The Broken Lantern Inn’s back room is narrow, its pine boards grey with age. A single sun-shaft slips through the warped shutter and lands on your blanket, stirring dust that tastes of last night’s hearth.

A clay cup waits on the stool beside you. You reach; the cup skates an inch across the wood, then stops. You stare, throat dry, but it sits ordinary as crockery can be. Somewhere below, the innkeeper haggles over the price of eggs.

Outside, wheels clatter and a woman laughs as her stall collapses with a clap of wood. The market is waking.

